## The Danger of Unchecked Indices

```xiom
fn get_element(arr: &Vec[Int], index: Int) -> Int {
  return arr[index];  // What if index is -1? What if index >= len?
}
```

### Contracts to the Rescue
```xiom
fn get_element(arr: &Vec[Int], index: Int) -> Int
  requires: index >= 0;
  requires: index < arr.len();
{ return arr[index]; }
```

### Set with Bounds Checking
```xiom
fn set_element(arr: &mut Vec[Int], index: Int, value: Int)
  requires: index >= 0;
  requires: index < arr.len();
  ensures: arr[index] == value;
{ arr[index] = value; }
```

### Slice Bounds
```xiom
fn slice[T](arr: &Vec[T], start: Int, end: Int) -> &Vec[T]
  requires: start >= 0;
  requires: end <= arr.len();
  requires: start <= end;
{ /* safe to slice arr[start..end] */ }
```

### 2D Array Bounds
```xiom
fn get_cell(grid: &Vec[Vec[Int]], row: Int, col: Int) -> Int
  requires: row >= 0 && row < grid.len();
  requires: col >= 0 && col < grid[row].len();
{ return grid[row][col]; }
```

### The Pattern
Every time you write array[index], ask: "Is index guaranteed to be in bounds?" If not, add a requires.

